  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{\u2032}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{\u2032}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is $R=4.$",
